Chapter: PortIn32Width
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 A 32-bit `in` writes EAX, and on x86-64 any 32-bit register write
 zero-extends into RAX. So `port-in-32` can never answer a negative
 number, whatever the device put on the bus, and a PCI vendor-and-device
 dword for an Intel part is exactly the case that tests it: vendor 0x8086
 with a device id of 0x8000 or above sets bit 31.

 This reads the dword RAW, without `pci-config-read-raw`'s mask, because
 the mask is what a guest does to defend itself and the question here is
 what the machine handed it in the first place. Masked, the answer is the
 same on a correct machine and a broken one, which is the definition of an
 arm that cannot fail.

 It exists because codex-vm got this wrong until 2026-07-30 and nothing
 could see it. The emulator computed the port result into a signed `int`
 and assigned it to a 64-bit register with no mask, while its own 8-bit
 and 16-bit arms masked correctly, so `0x8C318086` reached the guest as
 `0xFFFFFFFF8C318086`. Two agents read the guest's emitted `xor eax, eax;
 in eax, dx` and correctly concluded the builtin zero-extends; a third
 measured a negative value coming out of it. Both were right, and the
 defect was one layer below the thing they were reading.

 It needs no sidecar and no flag, and the reason is worth stating because
 the first version of this file claimed otherwise. An ABSENT PCI slot
 answers `0xFFFFFFFF`, which is the bit-31 case, so every machine supplies
 it and the probe finds one on the default bus. `-xhci-intel` adds a real
 Intel part (0x8086:0x8C31) as a second instance, but it was never
 load-bearing: the arm ran clean without it, which is how the claim was
 caught.

 The `bed not armed` branch below is therefore unreachable on any machine
 with an empty slot. It is kept because a bus with no bit-31 dword at all
 would make every other row vacuous, and a probe that cannot say "I was
 never armed" reports the same thing whether it passed or never ran.

Section: Probe

 Walks device slots on bus 0 and answers the raw reading for the first
 dword whose low half is a vendor id and whose high half sets bit 31.

  raw-at : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  raw-at (dev) = act
    w <- port-out-32 pci-config-addr (pci-address 0 dev 0 0)
    v <- port-in-32 pci-config-data
    v
  end

 Answers the first raw dword with bit 31 set, or zero if the bus carries
 none. Self-recursive so the loop is a tail call.

  scan : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  scan (dev) (found) = act
    if dev >= 32 then found
    else act
      v <- raw-at dev
      n <- scan (dev + 1) found
      if bit-and v 2147483648 /= 0 then v else n
    end
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing = act
    v <- scan 0 0
    if v == 0 then print-line-uni "no bit-31 dword on the bus: bed not armed"
    else act
      print-line-uni ("bit-31 dword found : yes")
      print-line-uni ("reads non-negative : " & (if v >= 0 then "yes" else "no"))
    end
  end

